window.QUIZ_BANK['git-github'] = (window.QUIZ_BANK['git-github']||[]).concat([
{id:3341,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What is the primary difference between git revert and git reset?',options:['git revert only works on remote repositories; git reset works locally','git revert creates a new commit that undoes changes, preserving history; git reset moves the branch pointer backward, rewriting history','git revert is faster because it skips the object database','git reset is safer for shared branches because it never alters history'],answer:1,explanation:'git revert is non-destructive — it creates a new commit that inversely applies the changes of a target commit, keeping all previous commits intact. git reset rewrites history by moving the HEAD and branch pointer back, which is dangerous on shared branches because it discards or moves commits that collaborators may have already fetched.'},
{id:3342,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What does git reset --soft HEAD~1 do?',options:['Discards all changes in the working directory permanently','Moves HEAD back one commit but leaves all changes staged in the index','Moves HEAD back one commit and unstages changes but keeps them in the working directory','Completely removes the last commit and all its changes'],answer:1,explanation:'git reset --soft HEAD~1 undoes the last commit but preserves every changed file in the staging area (index). This is useful when you committed too soon and want to revise the commit message or add more files before recommitting. No file content is lost — everything is still staged and ready.'},
{id:3343,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the behavior of git reset --mixed HEAD~1?',options:['Stages all changes from the reverted commit automatically','Moves HEAD back one commit, unstages the changes, but keeps file modifications in the working directory','Removes the commit and all associated file changes permanently','Identical to git reset --soft in all respects'],answer:1,explanation:'--mixed is the default mode for git reset. It moves the branch pointer back and clears the staging area, but the actual file changes remain in the working directory as unstaged modifications. You can then selectively re-stage and recommit parts of those changes, giving you finer control over what ends up in the new commit.'},
{id:3344,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does git reset --hard HEAD~1 do?',options:['Moves HEAD back one commit, keeps changes staged','Moves HEAD back one commit, unstages changes, but preserves working directory files','Moves HEAD back one commit and permanently discards all changes in both the index and working directory','Creates a revert commit that undoes the last commit'],answer:2,explanation:'git reset --hard is the most destructive reset mode. It moves the branch pointer back and wipes both the staging area and the working directory to match the target commit exactly. Any uncommitted changes or modifications in tracked files are permanently lost unless they exist elsewhere (e.g., in git reflog or a stash).'},
{id:3345,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'When should you prefer git revert over git reset on a shared branch?',options:['When you want to rewrite history to clean up messy commits','When the commits you want to undo have already been pushed to a remote branch that others are using','When you need to remove a commit from the very beginning of history','When git reset is not available in your Git version'],answer:1,explanation:'Once commits have been pushed to a shared remote branch, using git reset to remove them forces others to deal with diverged history and requires a force push. git revert is safe for shared branches because it adds new commits that undo the unwanted changes without rewriting existing history, avoiding conflicts for other contributors.'},
{id:3346,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What does git cherry-pick <commit-hash> do?',options:['Merges an entire branch into the current branch','Applies the changes introduced by a specific commit onto the current branch as a new commit','Copies a commit to a remote repository without a push','Selects the best commit from a list using an interactive prompt'],answer:1,explanation:'git cherry-pick takes the diff introduced by the specified commit and applies it on top of the current HEAD, creating a new commit with the same changes but a different hash. It is useful for backporting bug fixes to maintenance branches or applying individual features without merging an entire branch.'},
{id:3347,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How do you cherry-pick a range of commits from commit A (exclusive) to commit B (inclusive)?',options:['git cherry-pick A B','git cherry-pick A..B','git cherry-pick A...B','git cherry-pick --range A B'],answer:1,explanation:'The double-dot range notation A..B means "all commits reachable from B but not from A", which translates to commits after A up to and including B. For example, git cherry-pick abc123..def456 applies all commits between those two hashes in chronological order onto the current branch.'},
{id:3348,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does the --no-commit flag do when used with git cherry-pick?',options:['Skips applying the commit entirely','Applies the changes from the specified commit to the working directory and index but does not automatically create a new commit','Reverts the cherry-pick if there are any conflicts','Forces the cherry-pick without opening a conflict resolution window'],answer:1,explanation:'git cherry-pick --no-commit (or -n) applies the changes from the target commit into the staging area without committing. This lets you inspect or further modify the changes before committing. It is also useful when you want to cherry-pick multiple commits and combine them into a single new commit manually.'},
{id:3349,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What happens if a cherry-pick results in a merge conflict?',options:['Git automatically resolves the conflict using the source commit','Git aborts the cherry-pick and deletes all applied changes','Git pauses and marks conflicting files; you resolve them, stage the resolved files, then run git cherry-pick --continue','Git creates a merge commit to resolve the conflict'],answer:2,explanation:'When git cherry-pick encounters a conflict, it stops mid-operation and marks the conflicting hunks in the affected files with conflict markers. You resolve the conflicts manually, stage the resolved files with git add, then resume with git cherry-pick --continue. You can also abort the entire operation with git cherry-pick --abort to return to the pre-cherry-pick state.'},
{id:3350,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What is the purpose of git bisect?',options:['To split a large repository into multiple smaller ones','To perform a binary search through commit history to find the commit that introduced a bug','To merge two branches by bisecting their commit differences','To display a side-by-side comparison of two commits'],answer:1,explanation:'git bisect automates a binary search through commit history to locate the exact commit that introduced a regression or bug. You mark a known-good commit and a known-bad commit, and Git repeatedly checks out the midpoint commit for you to test. Each test narrows the range by half until the culprit commit is identified.'},
{id:3351,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the correct sequence to start a git bisect session?',options:['git bisect run, git bisect bad, git bisect good','git bisect start, git bisect bad <bad-commit>, git bisect good <good-commit>','git bisect open, git bisect mark-bad, git bisect mark-good','git bisect init, git bisect HEAD, git bisect origin/main'],answer:1,explanation:'You begin with git bisect start to enter bisect mode, then mark the known-broken commit with git bisect bad (defaulting to HEAD if no hash is given), and mark a known-working older commit with git bisect good <commit>. Git then checks out the midpoint commit so you can test it and continue narrowing down the culprit.'},
{id:3352,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How do you tell git bisect that the currently checked-out commit is bug-free during a bisect session?',options:['git bisect pass','git bisect ok','git bisect good','git bisect clear'],answer:2,explanation:'Running git bisect good while bisecting tells Git that the current commit does not exhibit the bug. Git then narrows the search to the range between this good commit and the most recent bad commit, checking out the new midpoint automatically. Continue marking commits good or bad until Git identifies the first bad commit.'},
{id:3353,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What does git bisect run <script> do?',options:['Runs the script only on the first and last commit in the bisect range','Automates the bisect process by running a script on each checked-out commit; the script exit code (0 = good, non-zero = bad) drives the bisect automatically','Executes the script once and uses its output as the commit range','Opens an interactive shell where the script is pre-loaded for each bisect step'],answer:1,explanation:'git bisect run fully automates the binary search. The provided script or command is executed on each candidate commit; if it exits with 0 Git marks the commit as good, if it exits with any non-zero code Git marks it as bad (exit code 125 marks a commit as untestable and skips it). This allows completely hands-free regression hunting in CI or long history ranges.'},
{id:3354,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'beginner',question:'How do you end a git bisect session and return to the original branch state?',options:['git bisect stop','git bisect end','git bisect reset','git bisect abort'],answer:2,explanation:'git bisect reset terminates the bisect session, restores HEAD to the branch tip you were on before starting bisect (or to the specified commit if provided), and cleans up bisect metadata. Always run this when done to avoid being left in a detached HEAD state pointing at some historical commit.'},
{id:3355,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What information does git blame show for each line of a file?',options:['The number of times a line was modified over its lifetime','The commit hash, author name, and date of the last commit that modified each line','A diff showing what the line looked like before and after the last change','The number of contributors who have touched the line'],answer:1,explanation:'git blame annotates each line of a file with the commit hash, author name, and timestamp of the most recent commit that changed that line. It helps you understand who introduced a specific piece of code and when, making it invaluable for code archaeology and tracing the origin of bugs.'},
{id:3356,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does the -L flag do in git blame?',options:['Limits the output to lines changed in the last N days','Restricts the blame annotation to a specific line range within the file','Lists only lines that were added, not modified','Shows blame for all branches, not just the current one'],answer:1,explanation:'git blame -L <start>,<end> restricts the annotation to a specific range of lines in the file, for example git blame -L 50,80 app.js. You can also use function names with git blame -L :functionName to annotate only the lines inside a named function. This makes it easier to focus on a specific section of a large file.'},
{id:3357,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does git blame --since="3 weeks ago" do?',options:['Shows only lines modified within the last 3 weeks','Limits the blame output to commits that are older than 3 weeks','Shows the entire file history up to 3 weeks ago','Filters out lines committed more than 3 weeks ago from the output'],answer:0,explanation:'git blame --since="3 weeks ago" restricts the annotation to show only commits that are newer than the given date for each line. Lines last touched before that cutoff appear attributed to an older boundary commit. This is useful for reviewing recent changes to a file without noise from historical modifications.'},
{id:3358,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What is git reflog?',options:['A remote log that records all pushes and fetches','A local log that records every position HEAD has pointed to, including commits, checkouts, resets, and rebases','A file that stores the authors of all commits in the repository','An alias for git log --all --decorate'],answer:1,explanation:'git reflog (reference log) is a local safety net maintained by Git that records every time HEAD or a branch tip changes, even for operations that rewrite history like rebase and reset. Entries expire after 90 days by default. It is the primary tool for recovering commits that appear to have been lost after a reset or abandoned rebase.'},
{id:3359,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How can you recover a commit that was accidentally lost with git reset --hard using git reflog?',options:['You cannot; git reset --hard permanently deletes commits from all logs','Run git reflog to find the commit hash of the lost commit, then git checkout or git reset --hard to that hash, or create a branch with git branch recover-branch <hash>','Run git fetch --all to restore lost commits from the remote','Use git stash pop to bring back the reset changes'],answer:1,explanation:'After a destructive reset, git reflog shows the previous HEAD positions including the commit that was "lost". You copy the hash of the commit you want to recover, then restore it with git reset --hard <hash> to move the current branch back to it, or with git branch new-branch <hash> to create a new branch pointing to it without moving your current branch.'},
{id:3360,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What does gc.reflogExpire control in Git configuration?',options:['The maximum size of the reflog file in kilobytes','The number of days before reflog entries are pruned by git gc; default is 90 days for reachable and 30 days for unreachable objects','The frequency at which git reflog compresses its entries','The number of references stored per reflog entry'],answer:1,explanation:'gc.reflogExpire (default 90 days) controls how long reflog entries for reachable commits are retained. gc.reflogExpireUnreachable (default 30 days) controls retention for unreachable commits. Running git gc or git reflog expire --expire=<date> prunes entries older than the cutoff. For critical repositories you can set these to longer values or never to prevent accidental data loss.'},
{id:3361,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is git worktree used for?',options:['Creating a backup copy of the .git directory','Checking out multiple branches simultaneously into separate directories from a single repository clone','Connecting two Git repositories so they share object storage','Splitting a monorepo into individual repositories'],answer:1,explanation:'git worktree lets you maintain several checked-out working trees from a single local repository. Each worktree has its own working directory and HEAD but shares the same .git object database and refs. This is ideal for reviewing a PR, hotfixing a release branch, or running tests on another branch without disturbing your current work or needing multiple full clones.'},
{id:3362,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'Which command adds a new linked worktree checked out to an existing branch named hotfix/1.2.1?',options:['git worktree create ../hotfix hotfix/1.2.1','git worktree add ../hotfix hotfix/1.2.1','git worktree checkout ../hotfix hotfix/1.2.1','git branch --worktree ../hotfix hotfix/1.2.1'],answer:1,explanation:'git worktree add <path> <branch> creates a new directory at <path> and checks out the specified branch there. The new worktree shares the same .git directory as the original. If the branch does not exist yet you can use -b to create it: git worktree add -b hotfix/1.2.1 ../hotfix origin/main.'},
{id:3363,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'beginner',question:'How do you list all worktrees associated with the current repository?',options:['git worktree status','git worktree ls','git worktree list','git branch --worktrees'],answer:2,explanation:'git worktree list displays all worktrees linked to the current repository, showing the absolute path of each worktree, its HEAD commit hash, and the checked-out branch name. The main worktree is always listed first followed by any linked worktrees added with git worktree add.'},
{id:3364,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does git worktree prune do?',options:['Removes all branches that have been fully merged','Deletes the .git/worktrees metadata for worktrees whose directories no longer exist on disk','Compresses the object database across all linked worktrees','Removes stale remote tracking references from all worktrees'],answer:1,explanation:'git worktree prune cleans up the administrative files inside .git/worktrees for any linked worktrees whose working directories have been manually deleted from the file system. Without pruning, Git still considers those paths as locked worktrees. Running prune or using git worktree remove properly handles cleanup.'},
{id:3365,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What is a Git submodule?',options:['A folder within a repository that is ignored by .gitignore','A pointer from one Git repository to a specific commit in another separate Git repository, allowing nested repos','A plugin system for extending Git commands','A branch that is set to track a specific remote'],answer:1,explanation:'A Git submodule embeds one Git repository inside another. The parent repository stores a reference (a .gitmodules file plus a special commit object) to a specific commit in the submodule repository. This allows a project to depend on specific versions of external libraries or components while keeping their histories separate.'},
{id:3366,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What command adds a submodule to an existing repository?',options:['git submodule init <url>','git submodule add <url> [<path>]','git submodule clone <url>','git include <url>'],answer:1,explanation:'git submodule add <repository-url> [optional-path] clones the specified repository into the given subdirectory (defaulting to the repository name), creates or updates the .gitmodules file with the mapping, and stages both the .gitmodules file and the new submodule directory entry. You then commit these changes to record the submodule in the parent repository.'},
{id:3367,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'After cloning a repository that contains submodules, what command initializes and fetches the submodule contents?',options:['git submodule fetch --all','git submodule update --init --recursive','git clone --submodules','git submodule pull'],answer:1,explanation:'By default, git clone does not populate submodule directories. Running git submodule update --init --recursive reads the .gitmodules file, registers each submodule, and then clones and checks out the pinned commit for every submodule (and any nested sub-submodules due to --recursive). Alternatively, using git clone --recurse-submodules handles this automatically during the initial clone.'},
{id:3368,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What does git submodule deinit <path> do?',options:['Permanently deletes the submodule directory and all its history','Unregisters the submodule by removing its config section from .git/config and clearing its working directory, but leaves .gitmodules untouched','Detaches the submodule from the parent repository and converts it into a regular directory','Stashes uncommitted changes inside the submodule before updating'],answer:1,explanation:'git submodule deinit removes the submodule entry from .git/config and empties the submodule working directory. The .gitmodules file and the commit pointer in the parent repository are not changed. To fully remove a submodule you also need to run git rm <path> (which updates .gitmodules and removes the tracked entry) and then delete the .git/modules/<name> directory.'},
{id:3369,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does git submodule status display?',options:['The commit hash of HEAD in the parent repository for each submodule','The SHA-1 commit hash each submodule is currently checked out at, prefixed with + if out of sync or - if not initialized','The remote URL of each submodule','The list of files changed inside each submodule'],answer:1,explanation:'git submodule status outputs one line per submodule showing the current checked-out commit SHA. A leading - means the submodule has not been initialized yet. A leading + means the checked-out commit differs from what the parent repository has recorded (the submodule is out of sync). No prefix means it matches exactly.'},
{id:3370,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is git subtree and how does it differ from git submodule?',options:['git subtree stores dependencies as symbolic links; git submodule stores them as commits','git subtree merges another repository\'s history directly into a subdirectory of the parent repo without requiring contributors to run extra commands; git submodule keeps the nested repo separate and requires explicit initialization steps','git subtree only works with local repositories; git submodule works with remote repositories','There is no difference — both commands are aliases for the same operation'],answer:1,explanation:'git subtree integrates a sub-project by importing its history into a subdirectory of the parent repository. Contributors do not need to know about the subtree — it looks like a normal directory. In contrast, submodules keep the child repository separate and require every developer to run git submodule update. Subtrees are simpler to use daily but make the parent repo history larger.'},
{id:3371,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'Which command adds a remote repository as a subtree into the prefix directory libs/utils?',options:['git subtree clone --prefix=libs/utils <url> main','git subtree add --prefix=libs/utils <remote> main --squash','git subtree insert --prefix=libs/utils <url>','git subtree init --prefix=libs/utils <remote>'],answer:1,explanation:'git subtree add --prefix=<dir> <remote-or-url> <branch> --squash merges the entire history of the specified remote branch into the given subdirectory. The --squash flag collapses the sub-project history into a single commit in the parent repo, keeping the parent log cleaner. Omitting --squash imports the full commit history of the sub-project.'},
{id:3372,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'advanced',question:'How do you push changes made inside a subtree prefix back to the upstream repository?',options:['git push subtree <remote> <prefix>:<branch>','git subtree push --prefix=<dir> <remote> <branch>','git subtree sync --prefix=<dir> <remote>','git remote push --subtree=<dir> <remote>'],answer:1,explanation:'git subtree push --prefix=<directory> <remote> <branch> extracts all commits that modified files inside the specified prefix, rewrites them as if they were made to the root of the sub-project, and then pushes those commits to the specified remote branch. This is the inverse of git subtree pull and enables bidirectional contribution.'},
{id:3373,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What are Git hooks?',options:['Remote webhooks that trigger when a push is received by a hosting service','Scripts placed in .git/hooks that Git automatically executes at specific points in the workflow, such as before a commit or before a push','Configuration files that define which branches can be pushed to a remote','Aliases for frequently used Git commands'],answer:1,explanation:'Git hooks are executable scripts stored in the .git/hooks directory. Git calls them automatically at defined lifecycle events. They can be used to enforce commit message formats, run linters, execute tests, block pushes, or trigger notifications. They are local by default and not committed with the repository, but tools like Husky help share hooks across a team.'},
{id:3374,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the pre-commit hook used for?',options:['Preparing the commit message template before the editor opens','Running checks on staged content before the commit is created — it can abort the commit by exiting with a non-zero status','Sending a notification after a commit is successfully created','Validating the remote repository before pushing'],answer:1,explanation:'The pre-commit hook fires just before Git creates the commit object, after the user runs git commit. If the script exits non-zero, the commit is aborted and the user is shown the output. Common uses include running linters, formatters, type checkers, or unit tests on staged files to prevent bad code from entering history.'},
{id:3375,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does the commit-msg hook receive and what is it used for?',options:['The list of staged files passed as command-line arguments','The path to a temporary file containing the commit message as its first argument; it validates or modifies the message before the commit is finalized','The full diff of staged changes on standard input','The remote URL and branch name for pre-push validation'],answer:1,explanation:'The commit-msg hook receives the path to the file containing the proposed commit message as $1. If the script exits non-zero, the commit is rejected. This hook is commonly used to enforce commit message conventions such as Conventional Commits, a Jira ticket reference format, or a minimum message length requirement.'},
{id:3376,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the pre-push hook used for?',options:['Preparing the local repository before fetching from a remote','Running checks before git push sends data to the remote — it can abort the push by exiting non-zero, preventing broken code from reaching shared branches','Automatically merging the remote branch after a successful push','Compressing objects before transmission to the remote server'],answer:1,explanation:'The pre-push hook fires after git push validates the remote but before any data is transmitted. It receives remote name and URL, and information about refs being pushed via stdin. Common uses: run the full test suite, check that no debug breakpoints were left, or enforce branch naming policies before code reaches the remote.'},
{id:3377,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'When does the post-merge hook execute?',options:['Immediately after git merge is run, regardless of whether a merge was needed','After a successful git merge (or git pull that results in a merge) completes, but it cannot abort the merge','Before conflicts are resolved during a merge','After a pull request is merged on GitHub'],answer:1,explanation:'The post-merge hook runs after a successful non-conflicting merge or after git pull completes a merge. It receives a flag indicating whether it was a squash merge. It cannot abort the merge since it runs after completion. Common uses: updating dependencies after a merge (npm install), reloading configuration files, or running code generation scripts.'},
{id:3378,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What is Husky (v8)?',options:['A GitHub Action for running security scans','A Node.js tool that makes it easy to manage and share Git hooks via the project repository, using a .husky directory for hook scripts','A graphical Git client for Windows and macOS','A linter for detecting common Git workflow mistakes'],answer:1,explanation:'Husky is a popular npm package that simplifies configuring and sharing Git hooks in JavaScript projects. In v8, hooks are stored as shell scripts in the .husky directory which is committed to the repository, so every team member gets the same hooks automatically after npm install. The setup is triggered by a prepare script in package.json.'},
{id:3379,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How do you install and set up Husky v8 in a Node.js project?',options:['npm install husky --save-dev then add "prepare": "husky" to package.json scripts, then run npm run prepare','npm install -g husky then run husky init globally','yarn add husky then add "postinstall": "husky install" to scripts','npm install husky-v8 --save then run npx husky setup'],answer:0,explanation:'The official Husky v8 setup: (1) npm install --save-dev husky, (2) add "prepare": "husky" to the scripts field in package.json, (3) run npm run prepare (or npx husky init) which creates the .husky directory and installs the Git hooks shim. After this, any scripts placed in .husky/<hook-name> are automatically executed at the corresponding Git lifecycle event.'},
{id:3380,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is lint-staged and why is it used with Husky?',options:['A tool that lints every file in the repository on every commit regardless of changes','A tool that runs linters and formatters only on the files that are currently staged for commit, making pre-commit checks fast','A CI service that lints pull request diffs on GitHub','A Git hook that counts the number of staged lines to enforce PR size limits'],answer:1,explanation:'lint-staged runs configured linters and formatters exclusively against files in the Git staging area. Combined with a Husky pre-commit hook, it ensures that only modified files are checked, keeping pre-commit feedback fast even in large codebases. A typical setup runs ESLint and Prettier only on staged .js/.ts files, preventing entire-project scans on every commit.'},
{id:3381,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the correct package.json configuration to run ESLint on staged .js files using lint-staged?',options:['"lint-staged": { "*.js": "eslint --fix" }','{"lint-staged": {"*.js": ["eslint --fix", "git add"]}}','"scripts": { "lint-staged": "eslint --staged" }','"husky": { "hooks": { "pre-commit": "eslint **/*.js" } }'],answer:0,explanation:'In modern lint-staged (v10+), the package.json lint-staged configuration maps glob patterns to commands. For example: "lint-staged": { "*.js": "eslint --fix" }. lint-staged automatically re-stages any files modified by the fix command, so the manual "git add" step is no longer needed. The hook is then triggered by Husky\'s pre-commit script running npx lint-staged.'},
{id:3382,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What is the GitFlow branching strategy?',options:['A strategy where all developers commit directly to main with no branches','A structured branching model using two long-lived branches (main and develop) and three types of short-lived branches: feature, release, and hotfix','A strategy using one branch per developer that is merged weekly','A CI/CD model where every commit to any branch triggers an automatic deployment'],answer:1,explanation:'GitFlow, popularized by Vincent Driessen, defines main (production code) and develop (integration branch) as permanent branches. Feature branches are forked from develop for new work and merged back. Release branches are cut from develop for stabilization then merged into both main and develop. Hotfix branches are forked from main for urgent fixes and merged into both main and develop.'},
{id:3383,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'In GitFlow, what is the naming convention for a feature branch?',options:['feat/feature-name','main/feature-name','feature/feature-name','dev-feature-name'],answer:2,explanation:'GitFlow convention uses the prefix feature/ for branches developing new features, e.g., feature/user-authentication or feature/payment-gateway. These branches are created from develop and merged back into develop when complete. The prefix makes it instantly clear what type of work the branch contains and is enforced by the git-flow CLI tool.'},
{id:3384,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'In GitFlow, from which branch should a hotfix branch be created?',options:['develop','feature/main-fixes','main (or master)','release/next'],answer:2,explanation:'Hotfix branches are always created from main (the production branch) because they need to fix a bug in the currently deployed code, not in the develop branch which may have unreleased work. The naming convention is hotfix/<version>, e.g., hotfix/1.2.1. After fixing the bug, the hotfix branch is merged into both main and develop to keep both branches synchronized.'},
{id:3385,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is GitHub Flow?',options:['GitHub\'s proprietary extension of GitFlow with release candidates','A simplified branching strategy using only main and short-lived feature branches, where every merge to main is deployed immediately','A workflow requiring weekly merges of all feature branches into a staging branch','A strategy identical to GitFlow but without the release branch'],answer:1,explanation:'GitHub Flow is a lightweight workflow: create a feature branch from main, make commits, open a pull request, review and pass CI checks, then merge to main and deploy immediately. It has no develop or release branches. It is ideal for continuous delivery environments where code is deployed frequently and teams want minimal branching overhead.'},
{id:3386,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is Trunk-Based Development (TBD)?',options:['A strategy where the main branch is called "trunk" and is never modified directly','A development model where all developers integrate small changes frequently into a single shared branch (trunk/main), using feature flags to hide incomplete work','A strategy where each developer maintains their own permanent branch','A branching model requiring daily merges from all feature branches into a release trunk'],answer:1,explanation:'Trunk-Based Development requires developers to commit small, frequent changes directly to the main branch (or through very short-lived branches of 1–2 days maximum). Long-lived feature branches are avoided. Incomplete features are hidden using feature toggles/flags. This minimizes merge conflicts and enables true continuous integration, and is commonly paired with advanced CI pipelines.'},
{id:3387,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is a feature flag and why is it important in Trunk-Based Development?',options:['A Git tag marking a feature branch ready for review','A runtime configuration switch that enables or disables a feature without deploying new code, allowing incomplete work to be merged to main safely','A GitHub PR label indicating the branch contains a new feature','A hook that prevents features from being deployed before they pass all tests'],answer:1,explanation:'Feature flags (also called feature toggles) are conditional code paths controlled by configuration rather than code deployment. In TBD, developers merge incomplete features behind a disabled flag so they do not affect production users. When the feature is ready, the flag is turned on. This decouples deployment from release, enables A/B testing, and allows instant rollback without reverting code.'},
{id:3388,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'Which statement best compares GitFlow, GitHub Flow, and Trunk-Based Development?',options:['All three are identical in structure and differ only in naming conventions','GitFlow suits release-scheduled software with complex branching; GitHub Flow suits teams doing continuous delivery; Trunk-Based Development suits high-velocity teams with mature CI/CD and feature flagging','GitFlow is the fastest workflow; Trunk-Based is the safest; GitHub Flow is for open-source only','GitHub Flow is deprecated in favor of GitFlow for all modern projects'],answer:1,explanation:'GitFlow excels for versioned software like libraries or mobile apps with distinct release cycles. GitHub Flow is a simpler continuous delivery model for teams that deploy main on every merge. Trunk-Based Development pushes this further by merging to main multiple times per day with zero long-lived branches, requiring excellent automated testing and feature flag infrastructure.'},
{id:3389,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What is the Conventional Commits specification?',options:['A convention that requires all commit messages to be written in a specific programming language','A standardized commit message format consisting of a type, optional scope, and description, designed to make commit history readable and automation-friendly','A GitHub policy that automatically formats commit messages after a PR merge','A set of rules for naming Git branches in enterprise projects'],answer:1,explanation:'Conventional Commits defines a structured commit message format: <type>(<scope>): <description>. Common types include feat (new feature), fix (bug fix), docs, style, refactor, test, and chore. This format enables automated changelog generation, semantic version bumping, and tooling like semantic-release to work deterministically from commit history alone.'},
{id:3390,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'In the Conventional Commits specification, what does a commit message with type "feat" trigger in semantic versioning?',options:['A PATCH version bump (e.g., 1.0.0 → 1.0.1)','A MINOR version bump (e.g., 1.0.0 → 1.1.0)','A MAJOR version bump (e.g., 1.0.0 → 2.0.0)','No version change — only documentation types trigger version bumps'],answer:1,explanation:'According to the Conventional Commits specification\'s mapping to SemVer: feat commits trigger a MINOR bump (new backward-compatible functionality), fix commits trigger a PATCH bump (backward-compatible bug fix), and a BREAKING CHANGE footer or a ! after the type triggers a MAJOR bump. This mapping allows automated versioning tools to calculate the correct next version purely from commit messages.'},
{id:3391,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is a BREAKING CHANGE in the Conventional Commits specification and how is it indicated?',options:['A commit that breaks the CI pipeline; indicated by the type "broken"','An incompatible API change indicated either by a "BREAKING CHANGE:" footer in the commit body or by appending ! after the type, e.g., feat!: remove deprecated endpoint','A change that modifies more than 100 lines of code','Any commit to the main branch that was not reviewed'],answer:1,explanation:'A BREAKING CHANGE indicates a change that is incompatible with the previous public API. It is communicated in two ways: (1) adding a footer line "BREAKING CHANGE: <description>" in the commit body, or (2) appending ! to the type/scope prefix, e.g., feat(api)!: change authentication flow. Both trigger a MAJOR version bump in automated release tools.'},
{id:3392,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the Conventional Commits format for a commit that adds a login feature affecting the auth module?',options:['ADD(auth) - login feature','feature: add login to auth module','feat(auth): add login feature','[auth] feat: add login feature'],answer:2,explanation:'The correct Conventional Commits format is type(scope): short description. Here: feat(auth): add login feature — "feat" is the type (new feature), "auth" is the optional scope (the affected module/component), and "add login feature" is the imperative-mood description. The scope is parenthesized and the whole prefix ends with a colon and space.'},
{id:3393,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What is semantic versioning (SemVer)?',options:['A versioning scheme based on the date of release in YYYY.MM.DD format','A versioning standard using three numbers MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH where each number increments according to the type of changes made','A GitHub feature that automatically assigns version numbers to tags','A strategy for naming Git branches based on the version of the software they target'],answer:1,explanation:'Semantic Versioning uses a three-part version number: MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH. MAJOR increments for incompatible API changes, MINOR increments for new backward-compatible functionality, and PATCH increments for backward-compatible bug fixes. For example, going from 2.3.1 to 2.4.0 means new features were added without breaking existing APIs.'},
{id:3394,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does a pre-release version identifier look like in SemVer?',options:['2.0.0-rc.1 where -rc.1 is the pre-release identifier appended after the patch version','2.0.0+rc1 where +rc1 is the build metadata label','2.0.0.rc1 using a fourth version segment','rc2.0.0 prefixing the pre-release label before MAJOR'],answer:0,explanation:'SemVer pre-release versions are denoted by appending a hyphen and dot-separated identifiers after the patch number, e.g., 1.0.0-alpha, 1.0.0-beta.1, 1.0.0-rc.2. Pre-release versions have lower precedence than the associated normal version. Build metadata is separate and uses a + sign, e.g., 1.0.0+20240101.sha.abc1234, and does not affect precedence.'},
{id:3395,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'advanced',question:'Under SemVer rules, when should the MAJOR version be reset to 0 and what does 0.y.z signify?',options:['MAJOR resets when you rename the project; 0.y.z means the project is archived','MAJOR starts at 0 for initial development (0.y.z); anything may change at any time and the public API is not considered stable until 1.0.0 is released','MAJOR resets on every calendar year rollover','0.y.z versions are reserved for internal/private packages only'],answer:1,explanation:'SemVer specifies that major version zero (0.y.z) is for initial development — the public API is unstable and breaking changes can occur in minor version increments. Version 1.0.0 marks the first stable public API. This allows projects to iterate rapidly before committing to API stability. Many open-source tools stay on 0.x for a long time while their API matures.'},
{id:3396,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the purpose of a CHANGELOG file?',options:['A log of all CI/CD pipeline execution times','A human-readable file that documents all notable changes made to a project for each version, grouped by release','A file listing all contributors sorted by number of commits','A configuration file that defines which commits trigger a release'],answer:1,explanation:'A CHANGELOG (often CHANGELOG.md) documents what changed between releases in a format useful to users and contributors. The keep-a-changelog format organizes entries under version headings with categories like Added, Changed, Deprecated, Removed, Fixed, and Security. It makes it easy to see what changed without reading raw git log output.'},
{id:3397,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does the standard-version tool do?',options:['Validates that all commits follow the Conventional Commits format before pushing','Automates version bumping, CHANGELOG generation, and Git tagging based on Conventional Commits in the commit history','Enforces SemVer compliance by rejecting non-conforming package versions in npm','Generates a release branch and creates a GitHub release automatically'],answer:1,explanation:'standard-version reads the commit history since the last tag, determines the next SemVer version based on Conventional Commits types (feat → minor, fix → patch, BREAKING CHANGE → major), updates package.json and CHANGELOG.md, creates a release commit, and creates a version tag. It is designed to be run locally before pushing; semantic-release does a similar job automatically in CI.'},
{id:3398,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is semantic-release and how does it differ from standard-version?',options:['semantic-release is a frontend GUI for standard-version; both work identically under the hood','semantic-release is a fully automated CI/CD release tool that runs in CI pipelines to determine version, generate CHANGELOG, publish packages, and create GitHub releases without any manual step; standard-version is a local-run helper that requires a manual trigger and push','standard-version supports more languages than semantic-release','semantic-release only supports GitHub while standard-version supports all Git hosts'],answer:1,explanation:'semantic-release is designed to run entirely in CI (e.g., GitHub Actions). It analyzes commits since the last release, decides the version bump, generates release notes, publishes to npm/other registries, and creates a GitHub Release — all without human intervention. standard-version is a lighter, locally-run tool that automates the CHANGELOG and tagging steps but still requires the developer to push the result.'},
{id:3399,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What are the best practices for code review regarding pull request size?',options:['PRs should always contain the entire feature regardless of how many files change','Smaller, focused PRs (ideally under 400 lines changed) are easier to review thoroughly, receive faster reviews, and result in fewer undetected defects','PRs should always contain at least one week of work to be worth reviewing','Large PRs receive better reviews because reviewers have more context'],answer:1,explanation:'Research and industry experience show that reviewers can effectively inspect approximately 200–400 lines of diff before attention and defect detection decline. Keeping PRs small and focused on a single concern makes reviews faster, reduces reviewer fatigue, makes it easier to revert if needed, and encourages more frequent feedback loops between author and reviewer.'},
{id:3400,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What constitutes constructive code review feedback?',options:['Pointing out every issue without suggesting solutions to demonstrate thoroughness','Feedback that is specific, actionable, explains the reason behind the suggestion, uses questions rather than commands where appropriate, and distinguishes blocking issues from optional suggestions','Rewriting the author\'s code entirely in the review comment to show the correct approach','Approving all PRs quickly to avoid slowing down the team'],answer:1,explanation:'Effective code review feedback focuses on the code, not the author. It is specific (references exact lines), explains why a change is suggested, offers alternatives or asks clarifying questions, and categorizes comments (e.g., "nit:", "blocking:") so the author understands priority. Empathy and a collaborative tone encourage a healthy review culture and continuous improvement.'},
{id:3401,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What are GitHub protected branch rules?',options:['Rules that prevent anyone from creating branches matching a specific pattern','Settings on a branch that restrict how changes can be introduced, such as requiring pull request reviews, passing status checks, disallowing force pushes, and requiring linear history','A paid GitHub feature that encrypts branch content','Rules that automatically delete branches after 30 days of inactivity'],answer:1,explanation:'Protected branch rules (configured under Settings → Branches in a GitHub repository) allow repository administrators to enforce policies on important branches like main. Options include: require N approving reviews, dismiss stale reviews on new commits, require specific status checks to pass, require branches to be up to date, prevent force pushes, prevent deletion, and require signed commits.'},
{id:3402,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does the "Require status checks to pass before merging" protected branch setting do?',options:['Sends a status email to all team members before a merge proceeds','Blocks the PR merge button until all specified CI/CD checks (e.g., tests, lint, build) have reported a passing status on the latest commit','Runs the status checks after the merge automatically to verify correctness','Requires manual approval from a status checker bot before merging'],answer:1,explanation:'When this protection is enabled, GitHub prevents merging the PR until all listed status checks (such as GitHub Actions workflow jobs, third-party CI services, or custom checks) report success on the HEAD commit of the PR branch. You can also require "branches to be up to date" to ensure checks ran against a merged state with the base branch.'},
{id:3403,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does "Require linear history" do when enabled on a protected branch?',options:['Prevents any commits with a timestamp older than the latest commit on the branch','Disallows merge commits and requires all PRs to be merged using squash or rebase strategy, keeping the branch history linear','Enforces alphabetical ordering of commit messages on the protected branch','Requires every commit to have a linear diff with no binary file changes'],answer:1,explanation:'Requiring linear history prevents merge commits from being created on the protected branch. Teams must use squash-and-merge or rebase-and-merge instead of create-a-merge-commit. A linear history makes git log, git bisect, and revert operations simpler because the history is a straight line rather than a directed acyclic graph with merge nodes.'},
{id:3404,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What is a CODEOWNERS file in GitHub?',options:['A file listing all past contributors and their contribution stats','A file in the repository root (or .github/) that maps file or directory patterns to GitHub users or teams who automatically become required reviewers when those paths are changed in a PR','A file that grants SSH key access to specific files in the repository','A configuration file for GitHub Copilot defining code ownership boundaries'],answer:1,explanation:'The CODEOWNERS file (stored at the repo root, docs/, or .github/) uses glob-like patterns to map paths to one or more GitHub usernames or @org/team references. When a PR modifies a file matching a pattern, the corresponding owners are automatically added as requested reviewers. Combined with "Require review from Code Owners" branch protection, this enforces domain expert review.'},
{id:3405,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the correct CODEOWNERS syntax to assign @org/backend-team as owner of all files under the src/api/ directory?',options:['src/api/* = @org/backend-team','src/api/ @org/backend-team','@owner src/api/ @org/backend-team','require-review: src/api/ @org/backend-team'],answer:1,explanation:'CODEOWNERS syntax is <pattern> <owner(s)>. For example: src/api/ @org/backend-team assigns @org/backend-team as owner of everything inside src/api/. Multiple owners can be listed space-separated. The last matching pattern in the file wins (unlike .gitignore where the last match wins for different reasons). Owners must have read access to the repo.'},
{id:3406,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is a GPG-signed commit and what does it prove?',options:['A commit signed using a GitHub personal access token to prove it was created via the API','A commit cryptographically signed with a GPG private key, proving that the commit was made by the holder of that key and that the commit content has not been tampered with since signing','A commit that has been approved by at least two required reviewers in a protected branch','A special commit type only available in GitHub Enterprise that includes audit metadata'],answer:1,explanation:'GPG (GNU Privacy Guard) signed commits use asymmetric cryptography. The committer signs the commit object with their private key; anyone with the corresponding public key (uploaded to GitHub) can verify the signature. GitHub shows a "Verified" badge on signed commits. This prevents commit author impersonation since anyone can set git config user.email to any value without signing.'},
{id:3407,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'Which git config command sets a GPG key for signing commits?',options:['git config --global commit.gpgSign true','git config --global user.signingkey <KEY-ID>','git config --global gpg.program <KEY-ID>','git config --global sign.key <KEY-ID>'],answer:1,explanation:'git config --global user.signingkey <KEY-ID> sets the GPG key ID to use for signing. You also need git config --global commit.gpgsign true to sign all commits automatically (or use the -S flag per-commit). The KEY-ID is the long-form fingerprint or short ID of your GPG key, obtained with gpg --list-secret-keys --keyid-format=long.'},
{id:3408,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How do you create a signed commit using GPG?',options:['git commit -m "message" --signed','git commit -S -m "message"','git commit --gpg -m "message"','git commit -K <keyid> -m "message"'],answer:1,explanation:'The -S flag (capital S) tells Git to sign the commit with the configured GPG key. For example: git commit -S -m "feat: add payment module". If commit.gpgsign=true is set in git config, every commit is signed automatically without needing -S. GitHub displays a green "Verified" badge next to signed commits whose public key matches a verified email on the account.'},
{id:3409,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'advanced',question:'How do you sign a Git tag with GPG?',options:['git tag -s v1.0.0 -m "Release v1.0.0"','git tag --sign v1.0.0','git tag -G v1.0.0 -m "Release v1.0.0"','git tag v1.0.0 --gpg-sign -m "Release v1.0.0"'],answer:0,explanation:'git tag -s (lowercase s) creates a GPG-signed annotated tag. You must also provide a tag message with -m or the -F flag. To verify a signed tag, use git tag -v v1.0.0. Signed tags are important for release integrity — they prove that a specific source snapshot was reviewed and approved by the release manager who holds the signing key.'},
{id:3410,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How do you verify a GPG-signed commit in Git?',options:['git verify --commit <hash>','git log --show-signature or git verify-commit <hash>','git check-signature <hash>','git gpg --verify <hash>'],answer:1,explanation:'git log --show-signature shows the GPG signature status alongside each commit in the log output. For a single commit, git verify-commit <hash> prints the full signature check result. Git looks up the public key in the local GPG keyring; if the signer\'s key is not imported, the verification will fail even for valid signatures — the public key must be imported first.'},
{id:3411,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What are common branch naming conventions in Git projects?',options:['Branches must use only lowercase letters and underscores','Use descriptive prefixes like feature/, bugfix/, hotfix/, release/, chore/ followed by a short slug, e.g., feature/user-login or bugfix/fix-null-pointer','Branches should be named after the developer who owns them','Branch names must match Jira ticket IDs exactly'],answer:1,explanation:'Common conventions use type-based prefixes (feature/, bugfix/ or fix/, hotfix/, release/, chore/, docs/) followed by a kebab-case description or ticket ID, e.g., feature/PROJ-123-add-oauth or fix/broken-pagination. Consistent naming makes it easy to understand a branch\'s purpose at a glance, and many CI/CD systems use branch name patterns to trigger specific pipelines.'},
{id:3412,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is a release branch in GitFlow and what happens to it after release?',options:['A branch created from develop for final testing and version bumping; it is merged into both main and develop, then deleted','A long-lived branch that accumulates features over multiple sprints before deployment','A branch created from main for hotfixes that is merged back to main only','A branch used exclusively by the CI system for artifact publishing'],answer:0,explanation:'In GitFlow, release branches (release/<version>) are cut from develop when a release is ready for final QA. Only bug fixes are committed here — no new features. When stable, the release branch is merged into main (and tagged) and also merged back into develop to incorporate any fixes. The release branch is then deleted. This isolates release prep from ongoing feature development.'},
{id:3413,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is the main criticism of GitFlow in modern continuous delivery environments?',options:['GitFlow does not support remote repositories','The long-lived develop and release branches create large divergence periods, making integration painful and slowing down the path to production compared to simpler models like GitHub Flow or Trunk-Based Development','GitFlow requires paid tooling that is not available for open-source projects','GitFlow branches cannot be used with pull requests'],answer:1,explanation:'GitFlow\'s multi-branch model was designed for scheduled releases with versioned software. For teams practicing continuous delivery, the overhead of maintaining develop, feature, release, and hotfix branches simultaneously creates long integration gaps, frequent merge conflicts, and delayed deployments. Simpler models like GitHub Flow or TBD are preferred for web services that deploy multiple times per day.'},
{id:3414,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does the "Dismiss stale pull request approvals when new commits are pushed" branch protection setting do?',options:['Automatically merges the PR after each new commit if there is one approval','Removes existing review approvals when the PR author pushes new commits, requiring reviewers to re-approve the updated code','Notifies reviewers via email whenever new commits are pushed but keeps existing approvals','Prevents the PR author from pushing commits after the first approval is received'],answer:1,explanation:'This protection prevents a common bypass: author gets approval on a safe version of the code, then pushes a new commit with problematic changes without re-review. When "dismiss stale approvals" is enabled, any new push to the PR branch automatically invalidates all existing approvals, forcing reviewers to re-examine and re-approve the latest code before it can be merged.'},
{id:3415,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does git revert HEAD do?',options:['Deletes the last commit and all its changes permanently','Moves HEAD backward one commit like git reset --hard HEAD~1','Creates a new commit that is the inverse of the current HEAD commit, effectively undoing the last commit while preserving history','Opens an editor to modify the last commit message'],answer:2,explanation:'git revert HEAD generates a new commit whose changes are the exact inverse of the HEAD commit. For every line added by HEAD, the revert removes it; for every line removed, it adds it back. The original commit remains in history, and the new revert commit is appended. This is safe for shared branches because history is not rewritten.'},
{id:3416,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'advanced',question:'How does git revert handle a merge commit?',options:['git revert works identically on merge commits as on regular commits','Reverting a merge commit requires specifying which parent to use as the mainline with the -m flag, e.g., git revert -m 1 <merge-commit-hash>','git revert refuses to operate on merge commits and requires a different command','Merge commits must first be split into their constituent commits before reverting'],answer:1,explanation:'Merge commits have two parents. git revert needs to know which parent represents the "mainline" to compute the inverse diff. The -m flag specifies the parent number (1-indexed). Typically -m 1 is used when reverting a feature branch merge into main, selecting main (parent 1) as the mainline and undoing all the feature branch changes. This is critical when reverting an accidentally merged PR.'},
{id:3417,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the difference between git reset HEAD <file> and git restore --staged <file>?',options:['git reset HEAD <file> removes the file from the repository; git restore --staged <file> only unstages it','Both commands unstage the specified file, removing it from the index while keeping the working directory changes; git restore --staged is the modern recommended syntax introduced in Git 2.23','git restore --staged creates a backup of the file before unstaging; git reset HEAD does not','git reset HEAD <file> works on untracked files; git restore --staged works only on tracked files'],answer:1,explanation:'Both commands have the same effect: they unstage the file without modifying the working directory. git reset HEAD <file> is the traditional syntax, while git restore --staged <file> was introduced in Git 2.23 as a more explicit and readable command specifically designed for unstaging. Modern Git documentation recommends git restore for this use case.'},
{id:3418,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the purpose of git bisect skip?',options:['Marks the current commit as good and skips ahead two commits','Tells git bisect to skip the current commit because it cannot be tested (e.g., it does not compile), and Git will try an adjacent commit instead','Stops the bisect session and returns to the original branch','Skips all commits made by a specific author during the bisect session'],answer:1,explanation:'git bisect skip is used when the current commit cannot be tested — for example, it fails to compile due to an unrelated issue. Git marks it as untestable and selects a nearby commit to test instead. This prevents an untestable commit from blocking the binary search. When using git bisect run, scripts should exit with code 125 to indicate a skip.'},
{id:3419,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does the git worktree remove command do?',options:['Deletes the entire repository including the main worktree','Removes a linked worktree directory and its administrative data from .git/worktrees, similar to manually deleting the directory and running git worktree prune','Detaches the worktree from the repository and converts it to a standalone Git repo','Removes all files from a worktree without deleting the directory itself'],answer:1,explanation:'git worktree remove <path> safely removes a linked worktree. It refuses to remove worktrees with uncommitted changes unless --force is used. It deletes both the working directory at <path> and the corresponding metadata entry in .git/worktrees. The original (main) worktree cannot be removed with this command.'},
{id:3420,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does the "Require branches to be up to date before merging" protected branch option ensure?',options:['Ensures the PR branch name matches the protected branch naming pattern','Requires that the PR branch has been rebased onto or merged with the latest base branch commit before the merge button becomes available, preventing integration of code that has not been tested against recent changes','Automatically runs git fetch on the PR branch every hour','Prevents merging if the PR branch is less than 1 hour old'],answer:1,explanation:'This setting prevents merging a PR whose branch is behind the base branch tip. Without it, a PR could pass all checks against an outdated base and then introduce conflicts or regressions when merged against newer code. Enabling it ensures that status checks ran against the most current version of the codebase, increasing confidence in the merged result.'},
{id:3421,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What is git stash and when would you use it?',options:['A remote backup mechanism for committed code','A command that temporarily saves uncommitted changes (both staged and unstaged) to a stack so you can switch branches with a clean working directory, then restore them later','A way to archive old commits to reduce repository size','An alias for git commit -a that stages and commits all changes at once'],answer:1,explanation:'git stash saves the current state of the working directory and index to a stack of stashes and reverts the working directory to match HEAD. This is useful when you need to switch to another branch urgently but are not ready to commit your current changes. git stash pop later restores the most recent stash entry and removes it from the stack.'},
{id:3422,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How does git cherry-pick -x differ from a regular cherry-pick?',options:['It cherry-picks and then immediately pushes to the remote','It appends a "(cherry picked from commit <hash>)" note to the commit message of the new commit, making it traceable back to the original source commit','It applies the cherry-picked changes without creating a new commit','It reverses the commit selection order when picking a range'],answer:1,explanation:'git cherry-pick -x adds a trailing line to the commit message documenting the original source commit hash, e.g., "(cherry picked from commit abc1234)". This traceability is especially useful on long-lived maintenance branches where you need to audit which upstream fixes have been backported and which commits they correspond to in the main development line.'},
{id:3423,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the difference between git tag and git tag -a in terms of the objects created?',options:['git tag -a creates a tag that cannot be deleted; git tag creates a temporary tag','git tag creates a lightweight tag (just a pointer to a commit with no extra metadata); git tag -a creates an annotated tag (a full Git object with tagger, date, message, and optionally a GPG signature)','git tag creates a local tag only; git tag -a creates a tag that is automatically pushed to remote','There is no difference — both commands create identical tag objects'],answer:1,explanation:'Lightweight tags are simple named references (similar to branches) pointing directly to commit objects — they carry no extra metadata. Annotated tags create a dedicated tag object in the Git database storing the tagger name, email, date, and a tag message. Annotated tags can also be GPG-signed. Most release workflows use annotated tags because of the richer metadata and signing capability.'},
{id:3424,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does the git reflog expire command do?',options:['Resets the 90-day expiry timer for all reflog entries','Manually prunes reflog entries older than a specified time, e.g., git reflog expire --expire=30.days refs/heads/main','Lists all reflog entries that will expire in the next 24 hours','Converts expired reflog entries into permanent commits'],answer:1,explanation:'git reflog expire --expire=<date> removes reflog entries older than the specified date. You can target a specific ref (e.g., refs/heads/main) or all refs (--all). This is typically called automatically by git gc but can be run manually to free up space or to enforce a stricter retention policy. After expiry, truly unreachable commits become eligible for garbage collection.'},
{id:3425,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is the purpose of the .gitmodules file?',options:['A lock file that prevents concurrent git operations on submodules','A configuration file committed to the repository that maps submodule names to their remote URLs and local paths, used by git submodule commands to locate and manage submodule repositories','A file that defines which files inside a submodule are tracked by the parent repository','A generated file that stores the exact commit hash of each submodule, updated on every pull'],answer:1,explanation:'.gitmodules is a text file in the root of the parent repository, committed alongside the submodule directory entry. Each entry records the submodule\'s name, path (relative to the parent root), and URL. When another developer clones the parent repo and runs git submodule update --init, Git reads .gitmodules to know where to clone each submodule from.'},
{id:3426,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What happens when you run git submodule update without --remote?',options:['Fetches the latest commit from the submodule\'s remote and checks it out','Checks out the commit recorded in the parent repository\'s tree for each submodule, not necessarily the latest commit on the submodule\'s branch','Removes and re-clones all submodules from scratch','Runs git pull inside each submodule directory'],answer:1,explanation:'git submodule update (without --remote) checks out the exact SHA-1 recorded in the parent repository\'s commit for each submodule, putting each submodule in a detached HEAD state. To update submodules to the latest tip of their configured branch, you must add --remote. This distinction prevents unintended upgrades — the parent repo controls exactly which submodule version is used.'},
{id:3427,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the recommended way to install a Husky v8 pre-commit hook that runs lint-staged?',options:['Add "pre-commit": "lint-staged" to the husky section in package.json','Run: echo "npx lint-staged" > .husky/pre-commit after setting up Husky, making the script executable','Run: husky add .husky/pre-commit "npx lint-staged" (the v7/earlier API)','Create .git/hooks/pre-commit manually and add lint-staged invocation'],answer:1,explanation:'In Husky v8, hooks are plain shell scripts in the .husky directory. After running the Husky setup, you create the hook file: echo "npx lint-staged" > .husky/pre-commit and ensure it is executable (chmod +x on Unix). Husky v7 used husky add/set commands and a husky key in package.json, but v8 simplified the API to just shell scripts in the .husky directory.'},
{id:3428,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the correct scope of the Conventional Commits "chore" type?',options:['Used for any commit that does not produce a deployable change — such as updating build scripts, CI config, dev dependencies, or tooling — that does not modify production source or test code','Used exclusively for removing dead code from the codebase','Used for large refactoring tasks that span multiple files','Reserved for automated bot commits like dependency updates from Dependabot'],answer:0,explanation:'"chore" is used for maintenance tasks that do not affect the production code or its tests: updating package.json devDependencies, modifying CI pipeline files, updating .gitignore, running code generation scripts, or updating linting configuration. It does not trigger a SemVer version bump in automated release tools because it introduces no user-facing change.'},
{id:3429,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the difference between git fetch and git pull?',options:['git fetch and git pull are identical operations','git fetch downloads commits and refs from the remote but does not modify the local working directory or branch; git pull is git fetch followed by a merge (or rebase) to integrate the fetched changes into the current branch','git fetch is used for downloading files; git pull is used for downloading commits','git pull only works on the default branch; git fetch works on all branches'],answer:1,explanation:'git fetch retrieves new commits, branches, and tags from the remote and updates remote-tracking branches (e.g., origin/main) without touching your local branches or working directory. git pull calls git fetch and then merges (or rebases, if configured) the fetched changes into the current branch. Using git fetch followed by a manual merge gives you more control over integration.'},
{id:3430,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does "Require signed commits" as a GitHub branch protection setting enforce?',options:['Only commits signed with a company-specific certificate are allowed on the branch','All commits pushed directly or merged into the protected branch must have a valid GPG or SSH signature; unsigned commits are rejected','Commits must include a sign-off line (Signed-off-by:) in their message','GitHub Actions workflows must be signed with OIDC tokens before merging'],answer:1,explanation:'"Require signed commits" is a GitHub branch protection rule that rejects any push to the protected branch containing unsigned commits. Combined with GPG or SSH commit signing, it provides a cryptographic guarantee that commits on the branch were authored by authenticated contributors. Note: squash-and-merge and rebase-and-merge on GitHub create new commits signed with GitHub\'s key.'},
{id:3431,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What does the git log --oneline flag display?',options:['Only the first line of each commit message alongside the full commit hash','A condensed one-line summary of each commit showing the abbreviated hash and the first line of the commit message','Only the commit hash without the message','The commit message and changed file list on a single line'],answer:1,explanation:'git log --oneline outputs each commit as a single line: the abbreviated 7-character commit hash followed by the first line of the commit message. This compact format is useful for quickly scanning commit history. Combining it with --graph --decorate gives a visual branch topology overview.'},
{id:3432,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the significance of the "no force push" protected branch rule on GitHub?',options:['It prevents anyone from using git push -f to overwrite the branch history, protecting shared branch history from being rewritten unilaterally','It limits the size of each push to prevent performance issues','It prevents the branch from being pushed to at all except by the repository owner','It forces all pushes to go through a pull request review before taking effect'],answer:0,explanation:'Force pushing (git push --force) rewrites the remote branch history, overwriting commits that others may have fetched. On shared branches like main or develop, this can cause team members to have diverged histories and lose work. The "disallow force pushes" protection prevents this even from repository administrators, ensuring historical integrity.'},
{id:3433,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is git commit --fixup and how is it used?',options:['Creates a commit with the message "fixup! <original message>" intended to be automatically squashed into the referenced commit during an interactive rebase with --autosquash','A flag that automatically fixes merge conflicts when committing','Creates a fixup commit that is immediately and silently squashed into the previous commit','Amends the last commit without changing its message'],answer:0,explanation:'git commit --fixup=<original-commit-hash> creates a commit whose message is prefixed with "fixup! " followed by the original commit\'s message. When you later run git rebase -i --autosquash, Git automatically positions and marks this fixup commit for squashing into its target. This is cleaner than manually editing the interactive rebase list when adding forgotten changes to an earlier commit.'},
{id:3434,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the purpose of the prepare-commit-msg Git hook?',options:['Runs after the commit message has been submitted to validate its format','Fires after the editor opens but before the message is committed, allowing default message population — such as injecting a Jira ticket ID from the branch name or pre-filling a commit template','Fires before the commit is created to abort if no message is provided','Runs the linter to check staged files before the commit message editor opens'],answer:1,explanation:'The prepare-commit-msg hook fires after Git prepares the default commit message (from a template, merge message, etc.) and before the user\'s editor opens. It receives the path to the message file, the message source type, and optionally the commit hash (for amends). It is used to automatically insert metadata like a ticket number parsed from the branch name.'},
{id:3435,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does the keep-a-changelog format recommend for the "Unreleased" section?',options:['The Unreleased section should be empty and only filled at the time of release','A section at the top of CHANGELOG.md tracking all changes merged since the last release but not yet published, categorized into Added, Changed, Deprecated, Removed, Fixed, and Security','An Unreleased section is optional and only used for pre-release versions','The Unreleased section should contain only BREAKING CHANGE entries'],answer:1,explanation:'keep-a-changelog (keepachangelog.com) recommends maintaining an [Unreleased] section at the top of CHANGELOG.md that accumulates changes as they are merged. When a release is made, the Unreleased content moves into a new versioned section with the release date. This discipline ensures the changelog is always up to date and does not require a large batch update at release time.'},
{id:3436,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is the git subtree split command used for?',options:['Splits a large commit into multiple smaller commits','Extracts the history of a specific subdirectory from the repository and creates a synthetic branch containing only commits that affected that directory, used to publish a subdirectory as a standalone repository','Splits a repository into two equal halves based on commit count','Breaks a diverged branch into its original commits before a merge'],answer:1,explanation:'git subtree split --prefix=<dir> rewrites history, creating a new branch (or can output a commit hash) containing only the commits that touched files inside the specified directory, with those files moved to the root. This is the inverse of git subtree add — it allows you to extract a subdirectory\'s history and push it to a standalone repository.'},
{id:3437,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What does git tag -d <tagname> do?',options:['Deletes the tag from the remote repository','Deletes the specified tag from the local repository only; a separate push with --delete is needed to remove it from the remote','Deprecates the tag so it is no longer visible in git tag listing','Converts an annotated tag to a lightweight tag'],answer:1,explanation:'git tag -d <tagname> removes the tag reference from the local repository. To also delete it from the remote, you must push the deletion: git push origin --delete <tagname> or git push origin :refs/tags/<tagname>. This two-step process exists because tags are separate from branch tracking and must be explicitly synchronized.'},
{id:3438,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the purpose of the Conventional Commits "docs" type?',options:['Used for commits that add or fix documentation only, such as README updates, API docs, or inline code comments — without changing any source code logic','Used for committing database schema documentation','Used for any commit that touches a .md file regardless of content type','A mandatory prefix required on all commits pushed to the main branch'],answer:0,explanation:'The "docs" type is for documentation-only changes with no impact on production code or tests, e.g., docs: update README installation instructions or docs(api): add endpoint descriptions. Like "chore", it does not trigger a SemVer version bump. This clear separation of doc changes from code changes keeps the commit history clean and makes automated changelog categorization accurate.'},
{id:3439,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is the git replace command used for?',options:['Replaces all occurrences of a string in tracked files','Creates a replacement map in the Git object database, causing Git to substitute one object for another transparently, used for grafting history or replacing objects without rewriting','Replaces the most recent commit with a new one, similar to git commit --amend','Renames a branch and updates all references to it'],answer:1,explanation:'git replace creates a loose "replacement" reference that redirects object lookups from one SHA to another. This allows inserting history into a repository (grafting) without rewriting the object database. It is primarily used for specialized scenarios like joining two repository histories, replacing a large binary commit with a placeholder, or testing history with a mock commit without affecting the original.'},
{id:3440,topic:'git-github',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is the difference between a shallow clone and a full clone, and when would you use each?',options:['A shallow clone contains only binary files; a full clone contains only source files','A shallow clone (git clone --depth=N) downloads only the last N commits of history, reducing clone time and disk usage; a full clone downloads the entire history. Shallow clones are used in CI pipelines for speed; full clones are needed for operations like git blame, git bisect, or generating accurate changelogs','A shallow clone excludes submodules; a full clone includes them','A shallow clone creates a local-only repository that cannot fetch from remotes'],answer:1,explanation:'git clone --depth=N creates a truncated history containing only the most recent N commits, significantly reducing clone time and bandwidth in CI environments. However, shallow clones cannot support git blame on older code, git bisect beyond the truncation point, or accurate git describe. You can deepen a shallow clone with git fetch --unshallow when full history is needed.'}
]);
